WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.240 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Could the same pencil seem longer just because someone moved
the ruler?

00:00:04.240 --> 00:00:07.600 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Imagine two friends measuring it from different marks.

00:00:07.600 --> 00:00:12.577 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Before we trust a number, let us investigate the unit, the
scale and the starting point.

00:00:12.577 --> 00:00:17.172 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Our learning goal is to measure length accurately using
useful metric units.

00:00:17.172 --> 00:00:18.732 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Success criteria.

00:00:18.732 --> 00:00:21.772 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
I can choose a sensible unit and instrument.

00:00:21.772 --> 00:00:24.892 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
I can read a ruler from the correct starting point.

00:00:24.892 --> 00:00:28.429 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
I can combine metres and centimetres accurately.

00:00:28.429 --> 00:00:29.109 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Remember that a measure counts equal units.

00:00:29.109 --> 00:00:38.549 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Millimetres help with small details, centimetres suit a
pencil, metres suit a room, and kilometres suit a long
journey.

00:00:38.549 --> 00:00:41.029 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Choose a tool with useful markings.

00:00:41.029 --> 00:00:45.592 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Smaller units can give a more precise measurement, rather
than changing the object.

00:00:45.592 --> 00:00:50.232 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A ribbon begins at the two centimetre mark and ends at nine
centimetres.

00:00:50.232 --> 00:00:51.592 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Predict its length.

00:00:51.592 --> 00:00:57.981 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Count the equal one-centimetre intervals between those
marks, rather than reading the final label alone.

00:00:57.981 --> 00:01:00.861 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
There are seven centimetres between the marks.

00:01:00.861 --> 00:01:03.181 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Nine minus two equals seven.

00:01:03.181 --> 00:01:10.125 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Moving the ruler does not stretch the ribbon: subtract the
starting reading whenever the object is not lined up with
zero.

00:01:10.125 --> 00:01:14.405 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A display board measures two metres and thirty-five
centimetres.

00:01:14.405 --> 00:01:17.005 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
We want a single centimetre reading.

00:01:17.005 --> 00:01:23.112 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
One metre contains one hundred centimetres, so the two
metres account for two hundred centimetres.

00:01:23.112 --> 00:01:25.992 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Add the remaining thirty-five centimetres.

00:01:25.992 --> 00:01:30.552 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Two hundred plus thirty-five makes two hundred and
thirty-five centimetres.

00:01:30.552 --> 00:01:35.147 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The combined measure and the single-unit measure describe
exactly the same length.

00:01:35.147 --> 00:01:39.587 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Reading nine centimetres as the ribbon length ignores where
it started.

00:01:39.587 --> 00:01:41.667 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Count the space between the marks.

00:01:41.667 --> 00:01:46.161 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A different starting mark changes the subtraction, but not
the physical ribbon.

00:01:46.161 --> 00:01:50.641 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A strip starts at three centimetres and ends at eleven
centimetres.

00:01:50.641 --> 00:01:51.961 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
How long is it?

00:01:51.961 --> 00:01:55.100 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Look at the model, and keep your prediction in mind.

00:01:55.100 --> 00:01:55.660 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Eleven minus three is eight centimetres.

00:01:55.660 --> 00:02:03.865 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Check by counting eight equal intervals, or placing a
zero-start ruler beside the same strip.

00:02:03.865 --> 00:02:08.225 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Choose a useful unit, read the interval size and check the
starting mark.

00:02:08.225 --> 00:02:13.345 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
For a practical investigation, measure a pencil in
centimetres and millimetres.

00:02:13.345 --> 00:02:16.985 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Discuss which reading describes its length more precisely.

00:02:16.985 --> 00:02:19.505 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Reflect on which model helped you and why.

00:02:19.505 --> 00:02:23.572 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Now try the MathMagic practice, and explain your thinking to
someone.
